# Design — delivered requirements — {{ project_name }}

One line per requirement id that has been **built and merged**. Append only;
nothing here is ever edited.

## What this is for

A planner could not tell what had actually been built. `coverage.sh` says which
requirements a plan CLAIMS — and its own report says so in as many words:
"covered means PLANNED, not delivered and not verified." A plan's `status:`
field was the nearest thing to an answer and it is set by hand, so on a real
project every plan still read `status: draft` long after its work had shipped.

So a steward planning the next piece had no way to know which requirements were
already live, and nothing in the repository could tell it. That is what this
file answers, and it answers it from the one thing that cannot be stale: a
merged pull request.

**The driver writes it.** `.claude/scripts/record-delivered.sh` reads the
`feat/<slug>` pull requests that have MERGED into the run's base, maps each slug
to the plan carrying it, and appends that plan's `covers:` ids. Nobody types a
line here, which is the point — a hand-maintained record of what shipped is a
record of what somebody remembered to write down.

## Delivered is not finished

A delivered requirement stays a requirement. The system has to go on satisfying
it, and the day it stops is a regression. Nothing leaves the design because it
was built — that is [`DESIGN.oracle.retired.md`](DESIGN.oracle.retired.md), it
is a different question, and only the owner answers it.

## Format

One line each, and the id in backticks exactly as the design spells it:

    - `R<n>` — delivered — YYYY-MM-DD — PR #<n>, plan `<slug>`

